79
00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:21.619
Dustin: well, the fork choice, I guess, most literally, but this is an example of that. It seems a little odd for builders to suggest

80
00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:23.180
Dustin: something else.

81
00:10:23.740 --> 00:10:29.379
potuz: No, I think it's… it's… it's… this is not changing, even with this PR, so…

82
00:10:29.380 --> 00:10:47.770
potuz: builders don't really know what the proposer's view of head is. It might be different from them. So it does make sense for builders to already produce blocks on top of different heads, because the proposer, when they call them and say, I want to be… I want to get a bid, give me a bid for now, this is for direct connections.

83
00:10:47.770 --> 00:10:55.070
potuz: Then the builder would already have all of the possible questions that the proposer would ask already answered.

84
00:10:55.120 --> 00:11:03.790
potuz: So the proposer is the one that decides over what is proposing, and the builder needs to sort of, like, prepare different blocks in parallel to be able to

85
00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:05.620
potuz: To provide those blocks.

86
00:11:05.810 --> 00:11:10.920
potuz: Now, over P2P, we already allow the builder to submit different bits.

87
00:11:11.570 --> 00:11:24.680
potuz: And if there are proposers, if there are peers that have different views of head, they will propagate different bits. So that's why I prefer this, just propagate one.

88
00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:40.329
potuz: your best bit, just one, don't allow multiple bits, but you propagate the best bit that is compatible with your current head. So if there are viable heads, then bits will be propagated for different, for different, blocks.

89
00:11:40.850 --> 00:11:50.990
potuz: This PR takes a different instance in saying, even if it's not the best bid, you should propagate it, because the proposer might find it more useful.

90
00:11:51.580 --> 00:12:00.389
potuz: So this makes… makes it such a way that you are allowing more traffic over the P2P stack, which I think is inherently less safe.

